   "id": "h2-13",
   "name": "Fibre (non-starch polysaccharides): notion and sources"
  }
 ],
 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "Dietary fibre, also called non-starch polysaccharides (NSP), is the part of plant food that the body cannot digest. It is mainly the walls of plant cells (cellulose).\nIt is found only in plant foods; animal foods (meat, fish, eggs, milk) contain none.\nGood sources: whole grains (maize, millet, brown rice, whole-wheat bread), beans and other legumes, vegetables (green leaves, okra, carrots), fruits with their skins (guava, mango, oranges with the pith), roots (cassava, cocoyam) and nuts.\nRefining removes fibre: white rice and white flour have much less than brown rice and whole flour; peeling fruit removes some.",
  "checks": []
 },
 "worked_example": {
  "status": "draft",
  "vars": {
   "m": "white rice and stew"
  },
  "problem": "How can a meal of {m} be given more fibre?",
  "steps": [
   "Choose brown rice instead of white rice (refining removes fibre).",
   "Add vegetables or beans to the stew, and eat fruit for dessert."
  ],
  "answer": "Brown rice, vegetables or beans in the stew, and fruit.",
  "checks": []
 },
